    { heading: "What makes a coloring page right for a preschooler", body: "Two-to-five-year-olds color with their whole arm, not their fingers, so they need shapes as big as a playing card and lines thick enough to see where to stop. A busy scene with a crowd, a city and a sky full of detail just becomes scribbles. These pages keep each picture to one or two main things — a basket in the reeds, a smiling sun — so your child can finish a page and feel proud of it in ten minutes." },
    { heading: "The story in one sentence", body: "Preschoolers remember a story when it is short. Baby Moses: his mother hid him in a basket on the river, and God kept him safe. Noah’s animals: God told Noah to bring the animals into the ark, two by two. Jonah: Jonah ran away from God, and a big fish swallowed him, then let him out on dry land. Jesus and the lamb: Jesus told a story about a shepherd who goes looking for one lost lamb until he finds it. Jesus loves me: Jesus took the little children in his arms and blessed them. The sun, moon and stars: God made the sun for the day and the moon and stars for the night." },
